# Encodings from Mesa's NAK (src/nouveau/compiler/nak/sm70_encode.rs), the encoder of SM70 to SM89:
# Copyright © 2022 Collabora, Ltd.
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
#
# The SM86 (Volta to Ada, 128-bit) instruction encodings sm86_decode.c decodes. tools/decodetree makes
# decode-sm86.inc from this file (src/nx/CMakeLists.txt, cmake/decodetree.cmake), and sm86_decode.c defines a trans_
# function for each opcode. The decoder reads the low 16 bits of the instruction (-w 16), the left bit bit 15:
# 12-15 are the predicate, which the decoder ignores (----), and the rest the opcode:
#   an ALU instruction: bits 0-8 its opcode and bits 9-11 the form, which places its sources (sm86.h). An opcode has
#     a pattern for each form it takes: 001 b a register; 010 b a register at 64, c an immediate; 011 the same, c a
#     constant buffer; 100 b an immediate; 101 b a constant buffer; 110 b a uniform register; 111 c a uniform
#     register. The uniform datapath's instructions (bit 91 set, uniform registers) take 001, 010, 100 and 110.
#   any other: bits 0-11, the opcode.
# A pattern's name is the opcode's, SM86_<NAME> in the translator, whose list (SM86_OPS in sm86.h) gives the enum
# order, whether it is an ALU instruction and its display name. No two patterns overlap, which decodetree checks.
# NAK's encoder gives every opcode but these, which the captured programs of an Ounce game use and NAK does not emit:
# FSEL (0x008), R2P (0x004), I2FP (0x045, SM86+), DEPBAR (0x91a) and the surface loads and stores that take their
# handle from a constant buffer (SULD.P 0xb97, SULD.D 0xb99, SUST.P 0xb9b, SUST.D 0xb9d: the bindless codes less one,
# as TEX's 0xb60 is to 0x361). CuAssembler's sm_86 encodings, which nvdisasm named, show all of them but SULD.P
# (github.com/cloudcores/CuAssembler, CuAsm/InsAsmRepos/DefaultInsAsmRepos.sm_86.txt), and I2FP only from sm_86 on.

