# TEX, TLD, TLD4 and TXQ with the handle in a uniform register: the constant buffer codes with bit 10, the register at
# 40-45 in place of the constant buffer's reference, bit 91 set, the rest of the fields theirs. CuAssembler's sm_86
# records: "TEX.SCR.LL.DC R34, R8, R2, R6, UR4, 2D" (0x00000000099e4f08_3000040602227f60) and "TXQ R35, R20, UR12,
# TEX_HEADER_DIMENSION, 0x2" (0x00000000080002ff_00000c0014237f6f). One game's programs take the handle from memory:
# an LDG.64 of a table of handles, R2UR, then UR + 1 = UR >> 20, the sampler's index
TEX_U        ---- 111 101100000
TLD_U        ---- 111 101100110
TLD4_U       ---- 111 101100011
TXQ_U        ---- 111 101101111
SULD_P       ---- 101 110010111
SULD_P_B     ---- 100 110011000
SULD_D       ---- 101 110011001
SULD_D_B     ---- 100 110011010
SUST_P       ---- 101 110011011
SUST_D       ---- 101 110011101
SUST_P_B     ---- 100 110011100
SUST_D_B     ---- 100 110011110
SURED_B      ---- 001 110100000
# SURED with the handle in a constant buffer: no source names 0x59f. The bindless 0x3a0 plus 0x1ff, as 0x998 is to
# 0xb97, and its fields (the handle at 40-58, the dimension, ADD, U32) agree in a game's program
SURED        ---- 010 110011111
# SUATOM with the handle in a constant buffer: the bindless 0x394 plus 0x1ff, as SURED's 0x59f is to 0x3a0, with the
# bindless one's fields (NAK's: destination 16, coordinates 24, data 32, dimension 61, type 73, operation 87-90) and
# SURED's handle at 40-58. A game's fragment programs use it
SUATOM       ---- 010 110010011
# the warp's matrix instructions, CuAssembler's sm_86 encodings: HMMA (0x23c, A at 24, B at 32, C at 64), LDSM
# (0x83b, the address register at 24 and offset at 40-63) and MOVM (0x23a, the source at 24)
HMMA         ---- 001 000111100
LDSM         ---- 100 000111011
MOVM         ---- 001 000111010
SUATOM_B     ---- 001 110010100
SUATOM_CAS_B ---- 001 110010110
# control flow: convergence barriers (BSSY, BSYNC, BREAK, BMOV) in place of Maxwell's CRS stack
BRA          ---- 100 101000111
# CALL.REL (0x944), RET.REL (0x950) and BRX (0x949): CuAssembler's sm_86 encodings
CALL         ---- 100 101000100
RET          ---- 100 101010000
BRX          ---- 100 101001001
# BRXU (0x958): BRX with the index in the uniform register at 24, CuAssembler's sm_86 encoding
BRXU         ---- 100 101011000
UBRA         ---- 010 101000111
EXIT         ---- 100 101001101
BSSY         ---- 100 101000101
BSYNC        ---- 100 101000001
BREAK        ---- 100 101000010
BMOV_R       ---- 001 101010101
BMOV_B       ---- 001 101010110
# BMOV.32 B, imm (0x956): the barrier at 24-27, the immediate at 32-63, from the SM89 tables that nv_isa_solver made
# with nvdisasm (github.com/kuterd/nv_isa_solver); the same code with 28-29 set names a state register. BMOV.32 B, B
# or a state register (0xf55): the barrier written at 16-23, the source at 24-29, as CuAssembler's sm_86 record
# "BMOV.32 B15, MACTIVE" (0x1a0f7f55) shows
BMOV_BI      ---- 100 101010110
BMOV_BL      ---- 111 101010101
WARPSYNC     ---- 001 101001000
WARPSYNC     ---- 100 101001000
WARPSYNC     ---- 101 101001000
WARPSYNC     ---- 110 101001000
KILL         ---- 100 101011011
BAR          ---- 101 100011101
NOP          ---- 100 100011000
# ERRBAR (0x9ab): CuAssembler sm_86
ERRBAR       ---- 100 110101011
DEPBAR       ---- 100 100011010
NANOSLEEP    ---- 001 101011101
NANOSLEEP    ---- 100 101011101
NANOSLEEP    ---- 101 101011101
NANOSLEEP    ---- 110 101011101
